+# Contract: `logredact` command-line interface (amended)
+
+**Feature**: `002-phone-redaction-out-dir` | **Date**: 2026-11-14
+
+This document **amends** [`../../001-redact-log-pii/contracts/cli-contract.md`](../../001-redact-log-pii/contracts/cli-contract.md).
+Everything that document specifies and this one does not restate continues to hold unchanged
+(FR-023). The redaction rules are in [`redaction-rules.md`](./redaction-rules.md).
+
+---
+
+## Invocation (amended)
+
+```text
+python -m logredact [--count] [--out-dir DIR] [FILE ...]
+```
+
+Requires Python 3.11+ with `src/` on `PYTHONPATH`. No installation step.
+
+### Arguments
+
+| Argument | Cardinality | Meaning | Status |
+|----------|-------------|---------|--------|
+| `--count` | 0 or 1 | Print a redaction summary to stderr after all output. | unchanged |
+| `--out-dir DIR` | 0 or 1 | Write each FILE's redacted lines to a file of the same name in DIR instead of to stdout. | **new** (FR-009) |
+| `FILE` | 0 or more | Input file to read, in the order given. | unchanged |
+
+`--count`, `--out-dir`, and `FILE` arguments may be interleaved in any order. All of
+`--out-dir clean a.log b.log`, `a.log --out-dir clean b.log`, and `a.log b.log --out-dir clean`
+are equivalent; `--out-dir=clean` is accepted too (FR-009).
+
+### Input selection
+
+Unchanged — except that `--out-dir` with **no** FILE is a usage error rather than a read of
+standard input (FR-019).
+
+| Command line | Input read |
+|--------------|------------|
+| no FILE, no `--out-dir` | standard input |
+| one or more FILE | each FILE in order; standard input is not read |
+| the same FILE twice | read twice, producing its output twice — and, with `--out-dir`, written twice and counted twice |
+| `--out-dir` with no FILE | **nothing** — usage error, exit 2 (FR-019) |
+
+---
+
+## Streams (amended)
+
+| Stream | Without `--out-dir` | With `--out-dir` |
+|--------|---------------------|------------------|
+| stdin | read only when no FILE is given | never read |
+| stdout | every input line, redacted, in order | **only** the single line `wrote N files` (FR-010, FR-014) |
+| stderr | the `--count` summary, or a diagnostic | same |
+| DIR | — | one output file per FILE argument |
+
+**Encoding and line fidelity**: unchanged, and output files carry the **same** contract as
+stdout — UTF-8, undecodable input bytes round-tripped byte-for-byte, line terminators preserved
+exactly including CRLF and a missing final newline (FR-013).
+
+**Ordering**: stdout is flushed before anything is written to stderr. With both `--out-dir` and
+`--count`, `wrote N files` is on stdout and the summary follows it on stderr (FR-022).
+
+---
+
+## The `--out-dir` destination (new)
+
+| Rule | Requirement |
+|------|-------------|
+| Output file name | the FILE's own name with its directories removed: `logs/2026/app.log` → `DIR/app.log` (FR-010) |
+| Missing destination | DIR and every missing parent are created (FR-011) |
+| Existing destination | used as it is; files in it that this run does not write are untouched |
+| Existing same-named file | replaced — after the run it holds only this run's redacted lines (FR-012) |
+| Validation timing | DIR is created or confirmed usable, and every FILE is checked for a collision with its own output path, **before the first FILE is read** (FR-015, FR-020) |
+| On success | the single line `wrote N files` on stdout, where N is the number of FILE arguments processed (FR-014) |
+
+`N` counts FILE **arguments**, not distinct paths, and the wording never changes with the
+number: one file reports `wrote 1 files`.
+
+**Partial-output guarantee (FR-017a)**: however a run ends, DIR contains only complete output
+files. The output file for a FILE is not created until that FILE has opened successfully, and a
+partly-written output file is removed before the tool exits. Output files completed for earlier
+FILEs are left in place, except where a later FILE writes to that same path.
+
+---
+
+## The `--count` summary (amended)
+
+Exact form, one line, to **stderr**:
+
+```text
+emails=N ips=N cards=N phones=N
+```
+
+The three existing keys are unchanged, in their existing order, with `phones` appended as a
+fourth (FR-007). Every other property is unchanged: always all keys, always this order,
+single-space separated, `0` when none; one summary per run covering all sources together,
+written once after all output; suppressed when a read fails, when any `--out-dir` failure
+occurs, and when the output consumer closes early.
+
+Each `N` equals the number of replacements of that type actually made — equivalently, the number
+of placeholders of that type present in the output (SC-003).
+
+---
+
+## Exit status
+
+Unchanged: **0 or 2 and no other value** (FR-023, SC-006). The new failure modes below all reuse
+status 2 and are distinguished by their diagnostics, not by a new status.
+
+| Status | Condition |
+|--------|-----------|
+| `0` | every input was read to the end; or the output consumer closed early |
+| `2` | a FILE could not be opened or read; an unaccepted argument; **or** any `--out-dir` failure below |
+
+---
+
+## Failure behaviour (new modes)
+
+Every mode below writes a diagnostic to stderr naming the offending path **exactly as written on
+the command line**, prints no stack trace, prints no `wrote N files` line, prints no `--count`
+summary, and exits 2.
+
+### `--out-dir` with no FILE (FR-019)
+
+- A usage diagnostic stating that `--out-dir` requires at least one FILE:
+
+  ```text
+  usage: python -m logredact [-h] [--count] [--out-dir DIR] [FILE ...]
+  python -m logredact: error: --out-dir requires at least one FILE
+  ```
+
+- Standard input is **not** read; stdout is empty; nothing is created on disk.
+
+### DIR cannot be created or used (FR-016)
+
+- Covers an existing regular file at that path, permission denied on a parent, and any other
+  reason the directory cannot exist.
+- **No FILE is read** and no output file is written.
+
+### An output file cannot be created or written (FR-017)
+
+- Covers an existing directory at the output path, permission denied, and a write that fails
+  partway.
+- The diagnostic names the **output** path; processing stops; the destination is left in the
+  state FR-017a requires.
+
+### A FILE cannot be read, with `--out-dir` (FR-018)
+
+- Exactly as today: the diagnostic names the **input** FILE, processing stops, exit 2.
+- Additionally: no file named after the unreadable FILE is left in DIR, and output files already
+  completed for earlier FILEs remain complete and valid.
+
+### An output path is its own input (FR-020)
+
+- Detected for **every** FILE on the command line, in pre-flight, before anything is read.
+- The file is left exactly as it was, and **no** output file is written for any FILE — including
+  FILEs that appear earlier on the command line than the offending one.
+
+### Output consumer closes early (unchanged, FR-023)
+
+With `--out-dir`, the only thing written to stdout is `wrote N files`, so the existing quiet
+exit continues to apply to it: nothing on stderr, exit 0.
+
+---
+
+## Worked examples
+
+New and changed rows only; the 001 table still holds for everything else.
+
+| # | Command | stdout | stderr | Status |
+|---|---------|--------|--------|--------|
+| 1 | `echo 'call +14155552671 now' \| python -m logredact` | `call [PHONE] now` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 2 | `echo 'a@b.io +442071838750' \| python -m logredact --count` | `[EMAIL] [PHONE]` | `emails=1 ips=0 cards=0 phones=1` | 0 |
+| 3 | `echo '+1234567' \| python -m logredact` | `+1234567` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 4 | `echo '+1234567890123456' \| python -m logredact` | `+1234567890123456` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 5 | `echo 'pay +4111111111111111 now' \| python -m logredact --count` | `pay +4111111111111111 now` | `emails=0 ips=0 cards=0 phones=0` | 0 |
+| 6 | `echo '+4111111111119' \| python -m logredact --count` | `[PHONE]` | `emails=0 ips=0 cards=0 phones=1` | 0 |
+| 7 | `echo '+4111 1111 1111 1111' \| python -m logredact` | `+[CARD]` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 8 | `echo '+15551234567@example.com' \| python -m logredact --count` | `[EMAIL]` | `emails=1 ips=0 cards=0 phones=0` | 0 |
+| 9 | `python -m logredact --out-dir clean app.log web.log` | `wrote 2 files` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 10 | `python -m logredact --out-dir clean one.log` | `wrote 1 files` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 11 | `python -m logredact --out-dir clean --count a.log b.log` | `wrote 2 files` | `emails=.. ips=.. cards=.. phones=..` | 0 |
+| 12 | `python -m logredact --out-dir clean logs/2026/app.log` | `wrote 1 files` → `clean/app.log` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 13 | `python -m logredact --out-dir clean` | *(empty)* | usage naming `--out-dir` | 2 |
+| 14 | `python -m logredact --out-dir existing-file a.log` | *(empty)* | names `existing-file` | 2 |
+| 15 | `python -m logredact --out-dir clean good.log missing.log` | *(empty)* | names `missing.log`; `clean/good.log` complete, no `clean/missing.log` | 2 |
+| 16 | `python -m logredact --out-dir . app.log` *(in app.log's folder)* | *(empty)* | names `app.log`, which is unmodified | 2 |
+| 17 | `python -m logredact --out-dir out a.log out/b.log` | *(empty)* | names `out/b.log`; no `out/a.log` written | 2 |
+
+---
+
+## Stability
+
+The 001 stability list stands, amended: the summary now has **four** keys in the order
+`emails ips cards phones`, and `[PHONE]` joins the placeholder set. The `{0, 2}` exit-status
+set, the stream assignment of output versus diagnostics, the UTF-8 round-trip guarantee, and
+the literal form `wrote N files` may not change without a spec amendment.
+
+## Internal API (not a public contract)
+
+Unchanged: `src/logredact/` exposes nothing to third parties; only `python -m logredact` is
+public. The module split exists for testability and may be reorganised freely as long as this
+contract holds.
