+# Contract: redaction rules (amended)
+
+**Feature**: `002-phone-redaction-out-dir` | **Date**: 2026-11-14
+
+This document **amends** [`../../001-redact-log-pii/contracts/redaction-rules.md`](../../001-redact-log-pii/contracts/redaction-rules.md).
+The email and IPv4 rules are unchanged and are not restated. Only the new phone number type and
+the one amendment to the card rule appear here.
+
+---
+
+## Phone number (new)
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| **Placeholder** | `[PHONE]` |
+| **Counter** | `phones` |
+| **Pattern** | `\+\d{8,15}(?!\d)` |
+| **Validation** | none — the pattern is the whole rule |
+
+A phone number is a `+` immediately followed by **8 to 15 decimal digits inclusive**, with no
+decimal digit immediately after the last of them. The `+` is part of the match and is replaced
+along with the digits (FR-002).
+
+- **Unbroken digits only.** No internal spaces, hyphens, parentheses, or dots. `+1 415 555 2671`
+  and `+1-415-555-2671` are not phone numbers in this form (FR-002).
+- **All or nothing.** A `+` followed by fewer than 8 digits, or by more than 15, is left
+  entirely unchanged and is never partially redacted. The trailing `(?!\d)` is what makes a
+  16-digit run produce no match at all, rather than a `[PHONE]` glued to the leftover digit
+  (FR-003).
+- **A preceding digit does not disqualify it.** `5+14155552671` → `5[PHONE]`: what precedes the
+  `+` is not part of a phone number.
+- **The placeholder is inert.** Input already containing `[PHONE]` passes through unchanged and
+  is not counted (FR-006).
+
+### Boundary table
+
+| Input | Digits | Output | Counted |
+|-------|--------|--------|---------|
+| `+1234567` | 7 | `+1234567` | — |
+| `+12345678` | 8 | `[PHONE]` | `phones=1` |
+| `+123456789012345` | 15 | `[PHONE]` | `phones=1` |
+| `+1234567890123456` | 16 | `+1234567890123456` | — |
+| `+12345678901234567890123456789012` | 32 | unchanged, in full | — |
+
+---
+
+## Payment card (amended: FR-003a)
+
+The pattern, the 13–19 digit bound, and the Luhn check are unchanged. One exclusion is added:
+
+> An unbroken run of decimal digits whose first digit is immediately preceded by `+` is **never**
+> recognised as a payment card number, whatever its length or Luhn status.
+
+A `+`-prefixed unbroken digit run is therefore either a phone number or untouched; it is never
+`[CARD]`.
+
+**The exclusion applies only to unbroken runs.** A card candidate written with separators is not
+an unbroken digit run and keeps its current behaviour.
+
+| Input | Today | Amended | Why |
+|-------|-------|---------|-----|
+| `4111111111111111` | `[CARD]` | `[CARD]` | no `+`; unchanged |
+| `+4111111111111111` | `+[CARD]` | **unchanged, nothing counted** | FR-003a; 16 digits is also not a phone |
+| `+4111111111119` | `+[CARD]` | **`[PHONE]`, counted as a phone** | 13 digits is a phone, and the `+` starts one character earlier |
+| `+4111 1111 1111 1111` | `+[CARD]` | `+[CARD]` | separators — not an unbroken run |
+| `+1 415 555 2671` | unchanged | unchanged | separators; the digits fail Luhn either way |
+
+These two rows in bold are **the only input shapes whose existing output changes**, beyond the
+addition of `[PHONE]` itself (spec "Card-length digit run after a `+`").
+
+---
+
+## Resolution order (amended)
+
+One left-to-right pass over each line, one pattern, four alternatives in this written order:
+
+```text
+email | ip | card | phone
+```
+
+1. **Position first.** The candidate starting at the earliest position wins, and scanning
+   resumes after the text it replaced (FR-004). This — not the list order — is what resolves
+   `+4111111111119` as a phone: its `+` sits one character before the card candidate's first
+   digit.
+2. **Written order breaks a tie.** When two types start at the *same* position, precedence is
+   email → IPv4 → card → phone (FR-005). Phone is appended last, leaving the 001 order literally
+   unchanged.
+3. **Placeholders are never revisited.** Scanning resumes after the replacement, so a `[PHONE]`
+   the tool inserted cannot be matched or counted (FR-006).
+
+The only type a phone number can tie with is an email address, because neither an IPv4 address
+nor a card number can start at a `+`:
+
+| Input | Output | Counted as |
+|-------|--------|-----------|
+| `+15551234567@example.com` | `[EMAIL]` | email — both start at the `+`, email is written first |
+| `jane+15551234567@example.com` | `[EMAIL]` | email — it starts earlier, so it wins on position |
+
+---
+
+## Counts (amended)
+
+Four running totals, one per type, accumulated across every input in a run:
+
+```text
+emails=N ips=N cards=N phones=N
+```
+
+Each counts **replacements actually made**, not candidates seen. A candidate rejected by
+validation — an out-of-range octet, a failed Luhn, or a `+`-prefixed run under FR-003a — is
+returned whole and unchanged and increments nothing.
